A||||11||deceler8||2.5 engine EGR valve question...||||1||
Z||000000||deceler8||09-28-2000||06:23 PM||||Is the EGR valve on a '86 2.5 engine special, or is it used on a variety of cars ?<P>My valve will open manually, but not when the engine is revved, so I'm assuming it has a leak in the vacuum diaphragm (the valve is getting vacuum).<P>I just wanna go to the boneyard and grab a few...am I stuck pulling them off '86 2.5 cars or is this valve used on other cars ?||||reg||1
Z||000001||William Federle||09-28-2000||08:06 PM||||I'm working on a problem that might involve my egr valve too, so I'm not an authority on the subject - but I think it is supposed to open only when your engine isn't being revd.<BR>It opens, I think, when there is high vacuum, and that isn't when your engine is being reved.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-28-2000||09:15 PM||||it opens under fairly narrow conditions. they are in the haynes book I think. <P>I beleive all the EGR's are the same on all 2.5's. should be close enough.||||reg||1
Z||000003||deceler8||09-28-2000||10:28 PM||||In a normal EGR valve one can usually stick their finger and feel for movement in the diaphragm. Not in this particular valve, which is an aftermarket one with the "staked" washer" crap.<P>What I did do was insert a short piece of vacuum hose in the holes on the rear to detect movement, and while not very exacting, I didn't feel any. I was able to open the valve with this piece of hose while the engine was idling, and it did stumble.<P>So at least the passages aren't clogged.||||reg||1
Z||000004||HugGRRRFiero||09-29-2000||10:18 AM||||The EGR valve is something I'd probably replace with a brand new one, but if you can find a good one in the boneyard go for it.<P>I believe a new one will come with a new gasket too.<P>------------------<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">         <BR>         <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A>  <p>[This message has been edited by HugGRRRFiero (edited 09-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-29-2000||02:09 PM||||make sure the vac line isn't leaking and is connected where it belongs on the TBI unit. The VECI label shows where.<P>The unversal EGRs often have a little plastic elbo fitting on top. make sure it's not leaking there. I hate these things. I think next time I'll see if I can find an exact fit one thru ACDelco or someone. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||09-29-2000||03:18 PM||||I know for sure the vac line isn't leaking...I replaced it a year ago when I replaced the original, seized EGR.<P>There's also no elbow on the top of my aftermarket valve. I've always had reservations about this valve and the "staked washer". That's why I'm planning to get a couple EGR's out of the boneyard. An OE ACDelco valve is a bit pricey.<P>Could the staked washer be a part of the problem ? I'm absolutely sure I used the right one and it has stayed put (I checked). Just how does this washer make a difference ?||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||09-29-2000||08:47 PM||||The washer is what meters the exhaust flow thru the valve. Bigger washer hole = more EG when the valve is full open.<P>If the correct washer is staked in tight then it's setup like it should be.<P>What exactly makes you think thre is an EGR problem? Is the car not running right.<P>Have you read the haynes description of how the "negative backpressure" EGR works? The valve only opens durring specific conditions of both exhaust pressure and vacuum.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||deceler8||09-30-2000||09:44 AM||||Actually, car runs fine, but has a nasty spark knock at part throttle conditions. Timing is right at 8 degrees (checked on both #1 and #4), no vac leaks that I could find, thermac appears to be operating properly.<P>MAP is a question mark as it's probably original...could this theoretically be causing the problem ?<P>Also, just to be sure...on the Thermac air cleaner, which port on the sensor does the vac source connect to...the one with the tiny hole or the one with the big hole ?<P>I'm no expert on EGR valves, but it seems that my knock appears at operating conditions where the EGR should be opening. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Indiana_resto_guy||09-30-2000||10:45 AM||||A good way to check for a vacumn leak of the EGR valve is to get a clean piece of vac line and put it on the valve and use it like a straw. Apply vacumn to it while feeling for movement, (should pull up) hold the vac negative pressure and make sure it isn't bleeding off (it should maintain position). Keep pressure for at least 15 seconds. If it does bleed off, you have a bad diaphramn in the EGR valve.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dan||09-30-2000||06:32 PM|||| You ain't gonna believe this but the 2.5 is a higher compression engine than the 2.8.Try running a higher octane gas and see if the knock goes away.There isn't any anti-knock sensor on the 2.5.Let me know.||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||09-30-2000||07:57 PM||||I'd recheck for vacuum leaks. pull the plugs and check the gap and condition. consider replacing the TPS if it's old. It could be losing accuracy which will screw up the ECM caclulations. (Don't wast time with a used TPS) <P>You might also want to consider one of the 3 part decarbonizer kits. You can get those from Ed Parks or your local pontiac/GM dealer. I've never used one but other people have had good results. Carbon buildup increases compression and often carries hot spots either of which will cause knock.<P>MAP can cause problems with ignition. It's used for vacuum advance calculation among other things. You problem seems to be a constant RPM ranges tho so I'd doubt the MAP for that. (If you where having problems when you punch it I'd look at MAP for sure)<P>The manifold side of the thermac sensor is the port that is sealed when the thing is cold. I don't know off hand which one that is. (If neither seal when cold the thing is shot.)<P>The other one vents to the air when cold. That is to bleed off the door so it will reset.||||reg||1
